\relax
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {1}Table 1 --- Centerpiece: all metrics $\times $ encoder at step 700K}{1}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The encoder-routing pattern at one fixed step.} GearNet $\to $ best T-W (FID, fJSD-A, fS-A) on both datasets. MPNN $\to $ best quality (Des). T-D: GearNet reduces designable diversity on AFDB ($-35$, $-50$ \#Clust); MPNN-L9-AFDB preserves it ($+21$, the clean falsifier). Random control (PDB only): wins on T-W and S-W distribution-match metrics (fJSD-A, ssJSD2D, pwTM) but \emph  {hurts} quality (Des $-0.08$) and designable diversity (\#Clust $-23$, E\%des $-0.11$) --- a regulariser without quality benefit.\relax }}{1}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {2}Table 2 --- Post-trade-off snapshot at step 1.0M}{1}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {3}Table 3 --- Speedup: REPA reaches the baseline's best FID in $N\times $ fewer steps}{1}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {4}Table 4 --- Sampler-noise robustness: $\Delta $ across $\gamma $}{1}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Post-trade-off: every PDB REPA variant now loses on T-D \#Clust} ($-27$ to $-62$). T-W gains persist (REPA still wins FID and fS-A on most variants) but tertiary diversity within the designable subset collapses. \textbf  {AFDB L9-MPNN remains the falsifier} --- the only REPA variant in the table with a positive \#Clust $\Delta $ ($+6$), and the encoder $\times $ dataset combination that escapes the trade-off entirely. The contrast against Table 1's 700K snapshot makes the temporal nature of the trade-off explicit: REPA's T-W gain is durable, the T-D loss develops with training.\relax }}{2}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Acceleration is durable on AFDB, transient on PDB.} ``Baseline best'' = cumulative-min FID across baseline's training; ``REPA reaches'' = earliest step REPA's cumulative-min hits the baseline's best. On AFDB, baseline FID-AFDB floors at 386 throughout 1.7M; REPA-GearNet both reaches that floor in 100--200K AND keeps improving past it (282--313). On PDB, REPA-L9 leads at every step $\le $1.2M but the long-trained baseline matches and edges below by 1.6M (288). PDB framing: transient acceleration to REPA's plateau, not a ``reaches-baseline-final'' speedup.\relax }}{2}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {5}Table 5 --- Representation rank-order (PDB-trained, best layer)}{2}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {REPA's encoder-routed effects are sampler-invariant in the middle band ($\gamma {\in }[0.35, 0.5]$) and largely survive at the extremes.} AFDB shows the clean ODE designability floor ($\Delta $\nobreakspace  {}Des $=+0.16$) and durable FID gains across all $\gamma $. PDB at $\gamma {=}0$ shows a striking $-1225$ FID gain: the PDB baseline at $\gamma {=}0$ mode-collapses (high Des, but very high FID); REPA's distribution-match gain is correspondingly large. The earlier $\gamma {=}1$ distribution- match-collapse claim is retracted --- L9-MPNN actually \emph  {wins} fJSD-A at $\gamma {=}1$ ($-0.30$), and only $\gamma {=}0$ shows the GearNet-specific Des-loss (not visible for MPNN here).\relax }}{3}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Encoder rank-order on student representation quality, best-layer, mean across steps $\ge $700K, $n{=}256$ PDB.} \textbf  {The axis split mirrors the gen-side encoder-routing (Table 1):} MPNN-L9 wins per-residue probes (IF, dihedral); GearNet-L9 wins per-chain CATH probes. Random gives the smallest gain on every probe --- learned target needed. CATH absolute values use the cleantrain protocol (highest $n$); IF and dihedral use xclean (doubly-clean cross-DB val).\relax }}{3}}
